Found it. The history is clear and only 3 commits ever touched this method.

## Bug-Introducing Commits
BIC: 88ddbe5ac6ac5aa0f18ade50aa376b25546fb058

## Reasoning

**(1) Investigation:** I ran `git log -p -L` on the `onFoodLevelChange` method in `ArenaListener.java` to see its full history. Only three commits ever touched this method: the original 2012 commit that added it, commit `88ddbe5` from 2019-07-20, and the fix commit `fb5db89` from 2019-07-28.

**(2) Specific change:** The original 2012 implementation correctly guarded the whole block with:
```java
if (!(event.getEntity() instanceof Player) || !arena.inArena((Player) event.getEntity()))
    return;
if (lockFoodLevel)
    event.setCancelled(true);
```
This ensured the food-lock only applied to players actually in the arena. Commit `88ddbe5` ("Lock food level for players in the lobby or spectator area") restructured the method to add lobby/spectator locking, but in doing so dropped the `arena.inArena(p)` check from the `arena.isRunning()` branch, leaving just:
```java
if (arena.isRunning()) {
    if (lockFoodLevel) {
        event.setCancelled(true);
    }
}
```
This meant that whenever the arena was running and `lockFoodLevel` was true, food level changes were cancelled for **every player on the server**, not just those inside the arena — exactly the bug described in the fix commit.

**(3) Evidence:** The fix commit `fb5db89` diff re-adds precisely `arena.inArena(p) &&` to that same conditional, restoring the missing check that `88ddbe5` had removed. The `-L` history trace shows no other commits modified this logic in between, so `88ddbe5` is the sole commit that introduced the regression.

I'm confident in this conclusion given the direct, minimal, and traceable history of the affected method.

